        "text": "Looking at the configuration validation in `server_args.py`, I can identify a critical unsafe interaction:\n\n**Unsafe interaction: `--load-format` with `--quantization` choices**\n\nThe `LOAD_FORMAT_CHOICES` list includes `\"bitsandbytes\"` and `\"expert_pack\"` as load formats, while `QUANTIZATION_CHOICES` also includes `\"bitsandbytes\"` and `\"expert_pack\"` (though `expert_pack` is not in QUANTIZATION_CHOICES). \n\nThe specific unsafe interaction is:\n\n1. **`--load-format bitsandbytes` combined with `--quantization bitsandbytes`**: This creates a contradictory configuration where the system attempts to load weights in bitsandbytes format while also applying bitsandbytes quantization. This can lead to double-quantization or undefined behavior, potentially causing memory corruption or incorrect model loading.\n\n2. **`--load-format expert_pack`**: This is explicitly noted in the code comments as \"intentionally narrow\" and validated only for specific DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731 MXFP4 GGUF and KIMI-K3-MXP4-DERISKED-Q2_K-*.gguf models. However, there's no validation that prevents combining `--load-format expert_pack` with incompatible `--quantization` choices (like `\"fp8\"` or `\"awq\"`), which could lead to loading errors or silent model corruption.\n\nThe most dangerous interaction is **`--load-format bitsandbytes` with `--quantization bitsandbytes`** because:\n- The load format determines how weights are loaded from disk\n- The quantization method determines how weights are processed after loading\n- Combining both creates a double-processing path that isn't properly validated\n- This could lead to memory corruption, incorrect inference results, or crashes\n\nThe code comments even acknowledge this issue in the `LOAD_FORMAT_CHOICES` note: \"Exposing it as a CLI choice let users create contradictory combos (see `_handle_load_format`).\" This suggests the developers are aware of potential contradictions but the validation may not be comprehensive enough to catch all unsafe combinations.",
